From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Smith <scott@newtrostudios.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:30:14 -0400
Subject: remotesmith(0801): verified file-transfer finalization and
 failed-item reporting

AC-18, AC-48, DEF-6 (whole-product review finding 2). Upstream dropped
write errors on the controller (io_loop "to-do"), renamed the
.download staging file to the final name without checking it and
ignored rename failures, so a full disk could leave a partial file under
the final name with a "done" status.

- TransferJob::write records the first open/write/finalize failure,
  removes that file's staging data and refuses every later block, so a
  gap is never covered by the blocks after it.
- Finalizing a file (on switch to the next file and when the sender is
  done) flushes and syncs it, which surfaces deferred write errors,
  checks its length against the size the sender announced and only then
  renames it into place. Any failure removes the staging file and is
  returned. The protocol carries no content hash, so size is the check.
- Controller downloads: a write failure cancels the job at the sender and
  reports it failed with the file name and OS error; a Done that crosses
  the cancel is ignored, so a failed item never flips to done. Done runs
  the verified finalize before reporting.
- Host uploads (CM): NewWrite now carries each file's size; a write
  failure fails the job once and drops it; WriteDone reports an error
  instead of done when finalizing fails.
- When the sender reports an error, a fully received last file is kept
  (verified) and a partial one removed.
- A new (not resumed) transfer of a file removes its stale
  .download/.digest left by an interrupted or crashed transfer; a resume
  keeps them, as upstream's resume needs them.

Tests (libs/base, fs::tests): complete files renamed with mtime, short
file never finalized, short earlier file fails on switch and later blocks
refused, unwritable destination, rename failure, stale staging cleanup,
and an ignored macOS test that fills a 5 MB hdiutil disk image.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
